[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Unit Testing with JUnit5, MockK, and Turbine in Android"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern Android development heavily relies on reactive programming using Kotlin Coroutines and StateFlow. Testing asynchronous code and state emissions requires specialized tools. This tutorial covers how to test ViewModels and business logic using JUnit 5, MockK for mocking dependencies, and Turbine for testing Flow emissions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Dependencies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Add the required testing dependencies to your build.gradle.kts file. Ensure you are using JUnit 5 along with Turbine for Flows and MockK for mocking."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"testImplementation("org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-api:5.10.0")
testRuntimeOnly("org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-engine:5.10.0")
testImplementation("io.mockk:mockk:1.13.8")
testImplementation("app.cash.turbine:turbine:1.0.0")
testImplementation("org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-coroutines-test:1.7.3")"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up JUnit 5 Extensions for Coroutines"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because JUnit 5 handles extensions differently than JUnit 4, we need a custom extension to swap the Dispatchers.Main dispatcher during tests. This ensures that any code utilizing Dispatchers.Main safely runs on a TestDispatcher."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class MainDispatcherExtension(
    val dispatcher: TestDispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher()
) : BeforeEachCallback, AfterEachCallback {
    override fun beforeEach(context: ExtensionContext?) {
        Dispatchers.setMain(dispatcher)
    }
    override fun afterEach(context: ExtensionContext?) {
        Dispatchers.resetMain()
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing the ViewModel Test"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let us consider a UserRepository and a UserViewModel that exposes a StateFlow. We will use MockK to mock the repository and Turbine to test the emitted states sequentially."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@ExtendWith(MainDispatcherExtension::class)
class UserViewModelTest {

    private val repository: UserRepository = mockk()
    private lateinit var viewModel: UserViewModel

    @Test
    "when loadUser is called, state updates to Success"() = runTest {
        // Given
        val expectedUser = User("John Doe")
        coEvery { repository.fetchUser() } returns expectedUser

        viewModel = UserViewModel(repository)

        // When & Then
        viewModel.uiState.test {
            // Initial state
            assertEquals(UserUiState.Loading, awaitItem())

            // Trigger action
            viewModel.loadUser()

            // Success state
            assertEquals(UserUiState.Success(expectedUser), awaitItem())
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":13,"paragraph":"bullet_point","text":"JUnit 5 offers a modern testing framework with expressive annotations and extensions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"MockK provides idiomatic Kotlin mocking capabilities with support for coroutines via coEvery and coVerify."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Turbine simplifies testing Cold and Hot Flows by offering a clean awaitItem() API inside a test block."}]
